@anon.b08dd5f4f09c4be6245191ddd275a566.477 = private unnamed_addr constant [15 x i8] c"unnecessary-map", align 1
@anon.b08dd5f4f09c4be6245191ddd275a566.478 = private unnamed_addr constant [963 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for unnecessary `map()` calls with lambda functions.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AUsing `map(func, iterable)` when `func` is a lambda is slower than\0Ausing a generator expression or a comprehension, as the latter approach\0Aavoids the function call overhead, in addition to being more readable.\0A\0AThis rule also applies to `map()` calls within `list()`, `set()`, and\0A`dict()` calls. For example:\0A\0A- Instead of `list(map(lambda num: num * 2, nums))`, use\0A  `[num * 2 for num in nums]`.\0A- Instead of `set(map(lambda num: num % 2 == 0, nums))`, use\0A  `{num % 2 == 0 for num in nums}`.\0A- Instead of `dict(map(lambda v: (v, v ** 2), values))`, use\0A  `{v: v ** 2 for v in values}`.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Amap(lambda x: x + 1, iterable)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0A(x + 1 for x in iterable)\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe, as it may occasionally drop comments\0Awhen rewriting the call. In most cases, though, comments will be preserved.\0A", align 1
