@anon.b08dd5f4f09c4be6245191ddd275a566.489 = private unnamed_addr constant [856 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for any usage of `__cached__` and `__file__` as an argument to\0A`logging.getLogger()`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AThe [logging documentation] recommends this pattern:\0A\0A```python\0Alogging.getLogger(__name__)\0A```\0A\0AHere, `__name__` is the fully qualified module name, such as `foo.bar`,\0Awhich is the intended format for logger names.\0A\0AThis rule detects probably-mistaken usage of similar module-level dunder constants:\0A\0A* `__cached__` - the pathname of the module's compiled version, such as `foo/__pycache__/bar.cpython-311.pyc`.\0A* `__file__` - the pathname of the module, such as `foo/bar.py`.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport logging\0A\0Alogger = logging.getLogger(__file__)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport logging\0A\0Alogger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\0A```\0A\0A[logging documentation]: https://docs.python.org/3/library/logging.html#logger-objects\0A", align 1
